.IX Title "tstregex 3"
.TH tstregex 3 "2026-05-14" "perl v5.26.3" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
tstregex \- A Hybrid Regex Diagnostic Tool (single file Library module and command tool)
shows the longest Regular Expression match / highlight the rejected part
.PP
Example:
.PP
$ perl lib/tstregex.pm '/^[a\-z]*\ed{3}$/' 'abc123' 'abc12a'
.PP
abc123
.PP
abc12a (^[a\-z]*\ed{3}$)
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
# Example of command and its terminal output :
.PP
\&\fBExample:\fR
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`$ perl lib/tstregex.pm \*(Aq/^[a\-z]*\ed{3}$/\*(Aq \*(Aqabc123\*(Aq \*(Aqabc12a\*(Aq\*(C'\fR
.PP
abc123
.PP
abc\fB12a\fR (\f(CW\*(C`^[a\-z]*\*(C'\fR\fB\ed{3}$\fR)
.PP
# The bold parts above highlight the rejected string and regex token.
.SH "OPTIONS (CLI)"
.IX Header "OPTIONS (CLI)"
.SS "\-h \-\-help"
.IX Subsection "-h --help"
show that help..
.SS "\-v \-\-verbose"
.IX Subsection "-v --verbose"
shows key info on (un)matching..
.SS "\-d \-\-diag"
.IX Subsection "-d --diag"
Triggers the Enriched Diagnostic View. It displays:
\&\- The string with the failing part highlighted.
\&\- The exact token in the regex that caused the break.
\&\- A visual pointer (\f(CW\*(C`^\-\-\- HERE\*(C'\fR) aligned with the regex syntax.
\&\- Execution time (useful for spotting ReDoS/Exponential backtracking).
.SS "\-a \-\-assert"
.IX Subsection "-a --assert"
Misc: performs a huge test suite various a large collection of regexp tests with tstregex..
.SS ""
.IX Subsection ""
.SH "Perl Module SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "Perl Module SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 9
\&  use tstregex;
\&  my $ctx = tstregex_init_desc(\*(Aq/^\ed{3}/\*(Aq);
\&  tstregex($ctx, \*(Aq12a\*(Aq);
\&  if (!tstregex_is_full_match($res))
\&      {
\&      my $token = tstregex_get_fail_token($res);
\&      my $pos   = tstregex_get_match_len($res);
\&      print "Failure on token \*(Aq$token\*(Aq at column $pos\en";
\&      }
.Ve
.SH "API"
.IX Header "API"
.SS "tstregex_init_desc($raw_re)"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_init_desc($raw_re)"
Pre-parses the regex, handles delimiters (m!!, //, etc.), extracts modifiers (i, s, m, x), and prepares the nibbling steps. Returns a context hash.
.ie n .SS "tstregex($ctx, $string)"
.el .SS "tstregex($ctx, \f(CW$string\fP)"
.IX Subsection "tstregex($ctx, $string)"
Executes the diagnostic. Updates the context.
.SS "tstregex_is_full_match"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_is_full_match"
Returns match status of input string (\s-1BOOL 0 OR 1\s0)
.SS "tstregex_get_match_portion"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_match_portion"
Returns the matching portion in case of full match
(might be smaller than input string, depending on anchors..)
.SS "tstregex_get_match_len"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_match_len"
Returns the matching substring length
.SS "tstregex_get_fail_token"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_fail_token"
Returns the failing token in the regexp
.SS "tstregex_get_re_clean"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_re_clean"
Returns the matching regexp subpart
.SS "tstregex_get_re_raw"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_re_raw"
Returns the internal representation of the regexp
.SS "tstregex_get_prefix_offset"
.IX Subsection "tstregex_get_prefix_offset"
Returns the offset of the original regexp in the raw regexp
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\f(CW\*(C`tstregex\*(C'\fR is designed to solve the \*(L"Black Box\*(R" problem of Regular Expressions.
When a complex regex fails, Perl usually just says \*(L"No Match\*(R". This tool
identifies exactly \fBwhere\fR and \fBwhy\fR it failed by finding the longest possible
partial match.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.IX Header "EXAMPLE"
.Vb 3
\&  $ perl lib/tstregex.pm \*(Aq/^[a\-z]*\ed{3}$/\*(Aq \*(Aqabc123\*(Aq \*(Aqabc12a\*(Aq
\&  abc123
\&  abcB<12a> (B<^[a\-z]*>\ed{3}$)
.Ve
.PP
\&\fIThe tool highlights the part of the string where the match failed.\fR
.ie n .SS "The ""Nibbling"" Engine"
.el .SS "The ``Nibbling'' Engine"
.IX Subsection "The Nibbling Engine"
The diagnostic logic uses a \*(L"Nibbling\*(R" (grignotage) strategy:
.IP "1. Decomposition" 4
.IX Item "1. Decomposition"
The engine breaks down your regex into a hierarchy of valid sub-patterns (lexical groups, atoms, and quantifiers) from longest to shortest.
.IP "2. Longest Match Search" 4
.IX Item "2. Longest Match Search"
It iteratively tests these sub-patterns against the input string. It's not just checking if the start matches, but what is the \fImaximum\fR sequence of instructions the engine could follow before hitting a wall.
.IP "3. Failure Point Identification" 4
.IX Item "3. Failure Point Identification"
Once the longest matching sub-pattern is found, the tool identifies the very next token in your regex syntax. This is your \*(L"Point of Failure\*(R".
